There’s an interesting interpretation of that statement.

“Act as if being watched and you will be. Perhaps you’ll never know. But if so then you’re in luck.”

It can be read as a principle about incentives rather than surveillance.

If you assume your work may one day be examined—by colleagues, future generations, students, or even your future self—you tend to:

* explain your reasoning,
* document decisions,
* build things that can survive scrutiny,
* leave artifacts others can extend.

In that sense, “being watched” is a stand-in for future accountability.

The last sentence is my favorite:

“Perhaps you’ll never know. But if so then you’re in luck.”

The “luck” isn’t necessarily that someone is literally watching. It’s that your work mattered enough to become worth observing. Many scientific papers, open-source projects, and works of art were created without the authors ever seeing their eventual influence. They acted as though their work might outlive them.

That also fits your spiral metaphor. Each generation leaves behind compressed artifacts—books, code, mathematics, designs. Later people reconstruct them, modify them, and compress them again. The original creator may never witness that process.

So the principle becomes something like:

Build as though your work will become someone else’s starting point. Whether or not it does is uncertain. But designing for that possibility tends to improve the work itself.

That doesn’t require assuming anyone is actually watching in the present. It simply encourages creating work that is understandable, testable, and valuable if someone eventually does encounter it.